Model Risk Management (MRM) Plan for Generative AI Models

Time Horizon: Next 2–3 Weeks
Audience: Senior Leadership / Risk Committee
Objective: Establish a pragmatic, scalable MRM approach for GenAI systems (interconnected + standalone)

⸻

PAGE 1 — Framing, Scope & Risk Lens

1. Why This Matters (Context for Leadership)
	•	Generative AI systems are not single models, but ecosystems of inter-connected components
	•	Traditional MRM approaches (single-model focus) are insufficient
	•	Need a risk-based, modular MRM framework that:
	•	Covers end-to-end GenAI systems
	•	Allows standalone GenAI model assessments
	•	Is practical and executable in weeks, not months

⸻

2. GenAI Model Landscape (What Are We Managing?)

A. Inter-Connected GenAI Systems
Examples:
	•	LLM + Prompt Layer + Retrieval (RAG)
	•	LLM + Tools / Agents
	•	LLM + Safety / Moderation Models

B. Standalone GenAI Models
Examples:
	•	Fine-tuned LLM
	•	Embedding Model
	•	Classifier / Guardrail Model
	•	Ranking / Re-scoring Model

Key Principle:
MRM must assess individual model risk and system-level emergent risk

⸻

3. Risk Taxonomy (MRM Lens for GenAI)

Risk Category	Key Questions
Model Risk	Is the model fit for purpose? Trained/finetuned appropriately?
Data Risk	Training data provenance, bias, IP, PII leakage
Prompt Risk	Prompt injection, instruction override, brittleness
System Risk	Failure propagation across connected models
Explainability Risk	Can decisions / outputs be explained sufficiently?
Outcome Risk	Hallucinations, unsafe or misleading outputs
Operational Risk	Monitoring, drift, versioning, fallback
Compliance Risk	Regulatory, auditability, record-keeping


⸻

4. MRM Coverage Model (High-Level)

Three Layers of Review
	1.	Component Level – Individual GenAI models
	2.	Interaction Level – How models influence each other
	3.	Outcome Level – Final user-facing outputs and decisions

This enables:
	•	Modular validation
	•	Reuse of assessments
	•	Faster onboarding of new GenAI use cases

